# --- LaTeX build artefacts -------------------------------------------
# Everything the compiler writes beside the source. Sources that happen to
# look like artefacts are not here: .tex, .TEX, .bib, .BIB, .cls, .clo, .sty,
# .latexmkrc and Makefile all stay tracked. If a pattern below ever shadows a
# file that belongs in the repository, force it in with `git add -f`.

# The core run: pdflatex, lualatex, latexmk.
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.lof
*.lot
*.loe
*.lol
*.lox
*.los
*.thm
*.dvi
*.xdv
*.ps
*.fdb_latexmk
*.fls
*.synctex.gz
*.synctex
*.synctex(busy)
*.synctex.gz(busy)
*.pdfsync

# Bibliography: biber, bibtex, biblatex. A *-SAVE-ERROR file is biber's dump
# after a failed run, which is what two concurrent builds produce.
*.bbl
*.bcf
*.blg
*.run.xml
*-SAVE-ERROR
*.brf

# Index, glossary and nomenclature.
*.idx
*.ind
*.ilg
*.xdy
*.glo
*.gls
*.glg
*.glsdefs
*.acn
*.acr
*.alg
*.nlo
*.nls
*.nlg

# Beamer and beamerposter, which the posters under knowledge/publications/
# would write if anyone ran their Makefile.
*.nav
*.snm
*.vrb

# Package leftovers: todonotes, minitoc, listings, minted, sagetex, elsarticle.
*.tdo
*.maf
*.mtc
*.mtc[0-9]*
*.listing
*.pyg
_minted*/
*.sagetex.sage
*.sagetex.py
*.sagetex.scmd
*.spl

# TikZ and pgfplots externalisation.
*.auxlock
*.figlist
*.makefile
*.md5
*.dpth
*.gnuplot
*.table
svg-inkscape/

# Compiled output. No figure PDFs are tracked; if one is ever added,
# force it in with `git add -f path/to/figure.pdf`.
*.pdf

# --- patch-script backups (scripts/ write these before editing) ------
*.bak
# Dated backups of config files, e.g. .gitignore.bak-2026-10-21. These do not
# end in .bak so the rule above misses them, and a `git add -A` would sweep
# them into the repository.
*.bak-[0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]-[0-9][0-9]

# --- who is working here ---------------------------------------------
# Written by the agent on its first run in a fresh clone, from answers the
# person gives. Personal and per-machine: never committed, so every clone
# starts by asking. CONTRIBUTOR.template.md is the tracked shape.
CONTRIBUTOR.md

# --- the notebooks' virtual environment --------------------------------
# Created per clone from notebooks/requirements.txt; see notebooks/README.md.
# virtualenv drops its own .gitignore inside, but python -m venv does not.
.venv/

# --- PaperDoctor review tool (symlinked from ~/Documents/tex/paperdoctor) --
# The skills and tools are links to a local clone; the review output is
# regenerated per run and lives beside each paper so sessions can read it.
/tools
/.claude/skills
*/metadata/
*/reports/
*/display/

# --- private correspondence -------------------------------------------
# Letters and drafts to other researchers. This repository is public, and
# correspondence is between the maintainer and the named person: it is not
# the project's to publish, and a draft is not a sent letter. Kept on disk,
# never committed. Nothing from this directory has ever been committed, so
# ignoring it is sufficient and no history rewrite is owed.
notes/correspondence/

# --- extracts and scratch passages ------------------------------------
# Prose pulled out of a section to work on in isolation, and anything else
# parked beside the paper that main.tex does not \input. Never part of the
# built document; kept on disk, not committed. A .tex here has no
# \documentclass, so VimTeX will not mistake one for a second root.
paper1/extracts/

# --- rendered media ----------------------------------------------------
# Explainer videos and their preview frames. Large binaries that would sit
# in history for ever: the two films alone are 65 MB against a .git of 13 MB.
# They are reproducible, so the renderers in scripts/ are the source of
# record and the output is not kept. media/web/ is the exception below:
# those are small text files meant to be integrated into a page.
# Exclude the directory's CONTENTS, not the directory: git never descends
# into an excluded directory, so a bare `media/` would make the negation
# below unreachable and the web files would be ignored too.
media/*
!media/web/
